WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:06.480
Hello everyone, I'm Vova, and this is the presentation of my test assignment.

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.960
So here we have the knowledge base, with a chat that you can ask

3
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.280
about your documents. It opens with the chat window when you have documents.

4
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.483
When you don't, it opens with the documents window.

5
00:00:20.363 --> 00:00:23.163
So, first of all, let's go and upload a document.

6
00:00:24.363 --> 00:00:27.243
I made it possible to upload PDFs,

7
00:00:27.323 --> 00:00:29.723
and what's interesting is that it's uploaded on the front end,

8
00:00:29.883 --> 00:00:34.085
so it happens almost instantly. And what is sent to the back end is just

9
00:00:34.085 --> 00:00:37.765
the text, what we need for embedding, not the actual file.

10
00:00:38.645 --> 00:00:41.125
Once you create the document, it becomes searchable,

11
00:00:41.285 --> 00:00:45.925
available for asking. So this is an article I wrote that argues that

12
00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:49.168
agents should be used on the web, not in a console interface.

13
00:00:49.248 --> 00:00:53.808
So let's see: how should I use my agents?

14
00:00:54.128 --> 00:00:56.208
Let's see if it fetches anything.

15
00:00:59.088 --> 00:01:02.768
And while it fetches, to clarify: it does streaming when it comes to this.

16
00:01:03.088 --> 00:01:06.811
What it does now is embed the same prompt with the same embedding model,

17
00:01:07.051 --> 00:01:11.851
then compare it to the vector database with pgvector, feed the

18
00:01:11.851 --> 00:01:16.411
most similar chunks into the context, and answer with references to this context.

19
00:01:16.491 --> 00:01:19.851
So you can see that it gathered sources

20
00:01:20.293 --> 00:01:25.173
from different documents, so you can go there and check what the document

21
00:01:25.173 --> 00:01:28.213
is about. The design is pretty rudimentary,

22
00:01:28.293 --> 00:01:31.253
as you see. As the brief put it,

23
00:01:31.413 --> 00:01:33.573
I went for usable, not beautiful,

24
00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:36.053
so it can be rough around the edges in places.

25
00:01:37.456 --> 00:01:39.856
What else do we have here? We have the usage tab.

26
00:01:40.656 --> 00:01:44.016
And what's interesting is that it's all home-written by the agent,

27
00:01:44.096 --> 00:01:46.976
so nothing here is from an external package.

28
00:01:47.136 --> 00:01:50.096
And I think that while the functionality is small,

29
00:01:50.416 --> 00:01:53.339
it is fine to have it written by yourself,

30
00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:58.379
and then you can consider moving on to a third-party package when you

31
00:01:58.379 --> 00:02:01.499
need it. We also have the models page,

32
00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:06.619
which shows which models we use, and this is configured, as the brief

33
00:02:06.699 --> 00:02:09.062
asked, via the .env file.

34
00:02:09.381 --> 00:02:13.622
So now let's go there and see how it all works from the setup

35
00:02:13.622 --> 00:02:16.582
standpoint. So I deleted this from here.

36
00:02:16.742 --> 00:02:21.382
Let's stop the server. I will save it without any

37
00:02:21.382 --> 00:02:24.261
API keys, and we will run the bootstrap command,

38
00:02:24.624 --> 00:02:28.944
which is basically the only command you need to run when you clone the

39
00:02:28.944 --> 00:02:33.264
repo. It asks for the OpenAI API keys,

40
00:02:33.824 --> 00:02:37.024
and you can reuse the chat key, and it writes them there.

41
00:02:37.264 --> 00:02:40.304
If you need other providers, you can change this later.

42
00:02:40.587 --> 00:02:43.227
So after this, you go here.

43
00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:47.307
You can write something else here.

44
00:02:52.347 --> 00:02:55.227
What frameworks do we use?

45
00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.470
And just to make sure it still works.

46
00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:06.390
Yep. So, as far as the technical stack is concerned,

47
00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.030
we will discuss it in more detail in the call,

48
00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:13.632
but basically it's a Turborepo monorepo, with an API app

49
00:03:13.632 --> 00:03:19.232
on NestJS, an FSD-sliced front end

50
00:03:19.392 --> 00:03:24.272
on Next.js, and packages for all the shared contracts,

51
00:03:24.272 --> 00:03:28.272
types and stuff like this. Everything runs locally:

52
00:03:29.195 --> 00:03:31.675
Next.js, NestJS, Supabase,

53
00:03:31.835 --> 00:03:34.155
Supabase Auth, and all these kinds of things.

54
00:03:34.475 --> 00:03:37.755
So yeah, it would be cool to discuss it with you in the call

55
00:03:37.995 --> 00:03:39.195
in more detail.
